---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Whole House vs Portable Dehumidifier",
    "url": "/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Basement Dehumidifiers",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Crawl Space Dehumidifiers",
    "url": "/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifiers With Pump",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifiers for Garages",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-garage/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Will a Basement Dehumidifier Help the Whole House?",
    "url": "/will-a-basement-dehumidifier-help-the-whole-house/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Much Electricity Does a Dehumidifier Use?",
    "url": "/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-19", modified: "2026-10-19" });
const title = "6 Best Whole House Dehumidifiers: Ducted, Ventilating and Low-Temperature Picks";
const description =
  "The best whole house dehumidifiers from Aprilaire, Santa Fe, Honeywell and AlorAir, sized on realistic ratings, with install costs, ductwork and running cost explained.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "aprilaire-e100-whole-house-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Aprilaire E100 Whole House Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "Ducted or Standalone, 100 Pints/Day",
    "description": "The Aprilaire E100 is the unit we would put on the shortlist for most humid houses with forced-air heating and cooling. It replaced Aprilaire's long-running 1850, which retailer SupplyHouse lists as discontinued in March 2023 with the E100 as its direct replacement. Retailers list it at 100 pints per day at the AHAM rating point of 80°F and 60 percent relative humidity, and the spec sheet for the Wi-Fi E100W variant adds a second, more realistic figure of 85 pints per day at 73°F and 60 percent. That second number is the one to size against, because few houses sit at 80°F. Aprilaire's own sizing puts it at up to 5,500 square feet, while retailer Sylvane suggests 3,000 square feet as its own figure, and we would plan around the lower number. The E100W sheet lists 280 CFM of airflow, a 6.7-amp draw on a standard 120-volt circuit, a 64-pound weight and a cabinet of roughly 14 by 15 by 26 inches, with a dehumidifying inlet range of 50 to 104°F. It can be ducted into the return side of an HVAC system or run standalone in a basement with its own supply duct, and there are plug-in, hard-wired, caster and crawl space variants. The trade-off is the usual one for this class: you need a condensate drain, ductwork and in most cases a contractor.",
    "pros": [
      "Rated 100 pints/day at 80°F/60% RH and 85 pints/day at 73°F/60% RH, per the E100W spec sheet",
      "Direct replacement for the long-running Aprilaire 1850, per SupplyHouse",
      "280 CFM airflow on a 120-volt, 6.7-amp circuit",
      "Plug-in, hard-wired, caster, Wi-Fi and crawl space versions sold",
      "Compact enough to hang in a basement ceiling or sit in a utility room"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Coverage claims vary: 5,500 sq ft from Aprilaire against 3,000 sq ft suggested by Sylvane",
      "Needs a condensate drain and usually professional duct work",
      "Dehumidifying range starts at 50°F, so it is not a cold-space unit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a humid house with ductwork, the Aprilaire E100 is the default pick: 85 realistic pints a day, a long product lineage and enough variants to fit almost any mechanical room."
  },
  {
    "id": "santa-fe-ultra-v100-ventilating-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Santa Fe Ultra V100 Ventilating Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best for Tight New Homes",
    "category": "Ventilating, Ducted, 100 Pints/Day",
    "description": "Santa Fe's Ultra V100 is the newer replacement for the Ultra98, which SupplyHouse lists as discontinued in February 2026. The difference that matters is the ventilation port: the V100 can pull in a measured stream of outdoor air, filter it and dry it before it enters the house, which is exactly what very airtight new builds tend to need. Santa Fe's spec sheet rates it at 100 pints, or about 12.6 gallons, per day at 80°F and 60 percent relative humidity, and 82 pints at 73°F and 60 percent. The ENERGY STAR listing gives 85.62 pints per day, which lines up with the cooler rating rather than the headline. The manufacturer sizes it for up to 2,300 square feet with 10-foot ceilings, and Sylvane's higher 3,600-square-foot suggestion is the retailer's, not Santa Fe's. The spec sheet lists 385 CFM of blower airflow, a 150 CFM ventilation rating, 620 watts at 73°F, efficiency of 6.4 pints per kilowatt-hour at the AHAM point, and three round collars of 8 and 10 inches. It is a big unit: 101 pounds and over 39 inches deep, so plan the install location before ordering.",
    "pros": [
      "100 pints/day at 80°F/60% RH and 82 pints/day at 73°F/60% RH, per Santa Fe",
      "Built-in fresh-air ventilation rated at 150 CFM",
      "ENERGY STAR listed at 85.62 pints/day and 419 kWh/year",
      "6.4 pints/kWh at the AHAM rating point, per the spec sheet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Manufacturer sizing is only 2,300 sq ft at 10-ft ceilings",
      "101 pounds and over 39 inches deep",
      "Ventilation ducting adds design work and installation cost"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If your house is tight enough that it needs fresh air as well as dry air, the Ultra V100 does both jobs in one box, at the cost of size and a more involved install."
  },
  {
    "id": "honeywell-dr90a3000-whole-house-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Honeywell Home DR90A3000 Whole House Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best Transparent Ratings",
    "category": "Ducted, 90 Pints/Day",
    "description": "Honeywell's DR90A3000/U is the one to look at if you want a ducted unit and a spec sheet that tells you plainly how capacity falls as the air cools. Honeywell's data lists 90 pints per day at 80°F, 65 pints at 70°F and 47 pints at 60°F, all at 60 percent relative humidity, which is more honest guidance than a single headline number. The spec sheet rates it for up to 2,800 square feet; Resideo's product page shows a garbled larger figure that conflicts with that, and we would trust the spec sheet. It draws 5.3 amps at 120 volts, moves 320 CFM with no duct restriction and 215 CFM at 0.6 inches of static pressure, uses a standard MERV 11 filter and lists efficiency of 2.9 liters, or about 6.1 pints, per kilowatt-hour. A retailer lists the cabinet at about 14 by 19 by 32 inches. Honeywell markets it as ENERGY STAR rated. Like every unit here it needs a drain and ductwork, and its rating falls further than the headline suggests in a cool basement, which Honeywell itself makes clear.",
    "pros": [
      "Published capacity at 80°F, 70°F and 60°F, per Honeywell's spec sheet",
      "Rated for up to 2,800 sq ft",
      "Uses a standard MERV 11 filter",
      "5.3-amp draw on a 120-volt circuit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Capacity falls to 47 pints/day at 60°F, per Honeywell",
      "Resideo's product page shows a conflicting square-footage figure",
      "Needs ductwork, a drain and usually a contractor"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The DR90 suits buyers who want to size honestly: Honeywell tells you what it removes at 60, 70 and 80 degrees, so you can match it to your actual house."
  },
  {
    "id": "aprilaire-e070-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Aprilaire E070 70-Pint Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best for Smaller Homes",
    "category": "Ducted or Standalone, 70 Pints/Day",
    "description": "The E070 is Aprilaire's smaller sibling to the E100, and it is the better buy for a house that is humid but not large. Aprilaire's own store page describes it as a 70-pint-per-day unit for crawl spaces, basements and whole houses up to 2,200 square feet, and Sylvane suggests 2,000. One distributor's listing for the Wi-Fi E070W claims 4,400 square feet, which conflicts with Aprilaire's figure, so we would ignore it. Retailers list about 200 CFM of airflow, a 5.8-amp draw at 120 volts, a washable MERV 8 filter and a five-year warranty. The cabinet is slim at roughly 12.5 by 12.5 by 25 inches and about 56 pounds, which is what lets it slide into a crawl space or tuck into a basement ceiling bay. Drain fittings differ by listing, so check the hose length you will get. It is not a substitute for the E100 in a big humid house, but for a 1,500-square-foot ranch with a damp basement it is properly sized rather than oversized.",
    "pros": [
      "70 pints/day for homes up to 2,200 sq ft, per Aprilaire",
      "Slim 12.5 x 12.5 x 25 inch cabinet fits crawl spaces and ceiling bays",
      "Washable MERV 8 filter and a 5-year warranty, per retailers",
      "Lower 5.8-amp draw than the 100-pint class"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Too small for large or very leaky houses",
      "One listing's 4,400 sq ft claim conflicts with Aprilaire's own figure",
      "Drain hose length differs between listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a smaller house or a crawl space feeding the whole home, the E070 is sized for the job rather than the brochure, and it fits where bigger units cannot."
  },
  {
    "id": "santa-fe-ultra70-ventilating-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Santa Fe Ultra70 Ventilating Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best Compact Ventilating",
    "category": "Ventilating, Ducted, 70 Pints/Day",
    "description": "The Santa Fe Ultra70, formerly sold as the Ultra-Aire 70H, is the compact way to get ventilation and dehumidification together. Santa Fe's 2024 spec sheet lists 70 pints, or 8.75 gallons, per day at 80°F and 60 percent relative humidity, and 52.45 pints at 73°F and 60 percent; the owner's manual uses the lower figure as its capacity rating, and so would we. Retailers size it for up to 1,800 square feet. The spec sheet lists 150 CFM of blower airflow and an operating range of 49 to 95°F, and a retailer lists a 12 by 12 by 28 inch cabinet. It can take a fresh-air duct, which is the point of buying it over a plain dehumidifier. Filter ratings differ between sources, from MERV 11 to MERV 14 depending on the sheet and the package, so check which filter ships with the unit you order. It is the smallest unit on this list, best for tight, modest-sized homes rather than big older ones.",
    "pros": [
      "70 pints/day at 80°F/60% RH and 52.45 pints/day at 73°F/60% RH, per Santa Fe",
      "Fresh-air ventilation option in a 12 x 12 x 28 inch cabinet",
      "Sized for up to 1,800 sq ft by retailers"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Lowest realistic capacity on this list",
      "Operating range starts at 49°F",
      "Filter rating varies between sources"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Ultra70 is the compact answer for a tight, modest house that needs both fresh air and dry air, as long as you size it on its 52-pint realistic rating."
  },
  {
    "id": "alorair-sentinel-hdi90-dehumidifier",
    "name": "AlorAir Sentinel HDi90 Dehumidifier",
    "badge": "Best Low-Temperature Option",
    "category": "Basement/Crawl Space, Duct-Capable, 90 Pints/Day",
    "description": "AlorAir's Sentinel HDi90 is sold primarily as a basement and crawl space unit, not a dedicated whole-house machine, so treat it as the budget-minded option for houses where most of the humidity lives below grade. Retailers list 90 pints per day at the AHAM point of 80°F and 60 percent, about 198 pints at the 90°F and 90 percent saturation condition and 47.71 pints at the cooler DOE condition of 60°F and 60 percent. AlorAir's spec sheet gives a minimum operating temperature of 33.8°F, which is much lower than the ducted units above and makes it the better fit for a cold, damp basement. Listings give coverage up to 2,600 square feet, 210 CFM of airflow, an ENERGY STAR rating with a coefficient of 2.69 liters per kilowatt-hour, a sound level under 58 dB(A) and a weight of about 70.5 pounds. A duct-capable version is sold, which lets it push dry air up into the house through a dedicated supply. Model names on retail listings mix HD90 and HDi90, so confirm which you are buying.",
    "pros": [
      "90 pints/day at AHAM and 47.71 pints/day at 60°F/60% RH, per retailer listings",
      "Minimum operating temperature of 33.8°F, per AlorAir's spec sheet",
      "ENERGY STAR rated, with a duct-capable version available"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Marketed for basements and crawl spaces, not as a whole-house system",
      "Retail listings mix HD90 and HDi90 model names",
      "Louder than the ducted units, at up to 58 dB(A)"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If the humidity in your house starts in a cold basement, the HDi90 works lower down the thermometer than any ducted unit here, at a lower price than a full whole-house system."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Whole House Dehumidifiers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a whole house dehumidifier worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is worth it when the whole house is humid, not just one room. Equipment typically runs about $1,200 to $2,500 plus installation, so for a single damp basement a portable is usually cheaper. In a humid climate where every room feels sticky, one ducted unit replaces several portables and dries the house more evenly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a whole house dehumidifier cost installed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Equipment typically runs about $1,200 to $2,500. A standalone install with its own short duct runs commonly adds $500 to $1,200, and integrating it with the HVAC system commonly adds $800 to $1,500. These are typical ranges, not quotes, and local labor rates and duct complexity move them a lot."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size whole house dehumidifier do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Size on the capacity at about 73°F, not the 80°F headline, and on measured humidity rather than square footage alone. A house needing about 60 to 70 pints a day in midsummer usually wants a 100-pint-class unit. A contractor's load calculation beats any square-foot chart."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a whole house dehumidifier work without ductwork?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some can run standalone in a basement with their own short supply duct, which dries that space and some of the house above it. To dry every room evenly, though, the unit normally connects to the HVAC ducts. Basement-first units like the AlorAir HDi90 are sold for exactly this standalone role."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What humidity should I set a whole house dehumidifier to?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Around 45 to 50 percent relative humidity suits most homes. Below about 40 percent you are paying to remove moisture you do not need to, and above about 55 to 60 percent mold and dust mites thrive. Start at 50 and adjust after a few weeks of hygrometer readings."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much electricity does a whole house dehumidifier use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "ENERGY STAR lists the Santa Fe Ultra V100 at 419 kWh a year, and Santa Fe gives its draw as about 620 watts at 73°F. Multiply the wattage by your running hours and electricity rate for your own estimate. These units remove more water per kilowatt-hour than most portables."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do whole house dehumidifiers need a drain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. At 50 to 100 pints a day, a bucket is not practical, so the unit drains to a floor drain, utility sink or condensate pump. The drain line needs a steady slope, any trap the manufacturer requires and protection from freezing if it passes through an unheated space."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Basement Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "best whole house dehumidifier",
      "whole home dehumidifier",
      "ducted dehumidifier",
      "hvac dehumidifier",
      "whole house dehumidifier cost"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Whole House Dehumidifiers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Ducted, ventilating and low-temperature units for houses that are humid throughout, sized on their realistic ratings rather than the 80-degree headline.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#aprilaire-e100-whole-house-dehumidifier"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Ducted, Ventilating or Basement-First</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Honestly</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Installation and Ductwork</a>
          <a href="#controls" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Controls and Setpoints</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance and Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#match" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Match a Pick to Your House</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most humid houses with forced-air HVAC, the Aprilaire E100 is the whole house dehumidifier to price first. Tight new homes should look at a ventilating Santa Fe, and a cold, damp basement is better served by a low-temperature AlorAir. Whatever you buy, size it on the rating at 73°F or 60°F, not the 80°F headline every box leads with.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not install six whole house dehumidifiers in one building and run them through a humid summer, and we will not pretend otherwise. This guide is built from manufacturer specification sheets, ENERGY STAR listings, retailer pages that repeat the manufacturer's ratings and the way these units are specified by HVAC contractors. Every capacity, airflow and electrical figure on this page is attributed to its source, and where sources disagree we say so rather than picking the more flattering number.
          </p>

          <p>
            We looked for three things. First, a published capacity at a realistic condition as well as the headline. The AHAM rating point of 80°F and 60 percent relative humidity flatters every unit, and the manufacturers that also publish a figure at 73°F or 60°F gave us something honest to size against. Second, a believable square-footage rating. Several units carry one coverage figure from the manufacturer and a different one from a retailer, and we used the manufacturer's whenever we had it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, we wanted range. Some houses are humid because they are tight and need fresh air as well, some because a basement or crawl space is pumping moisture into the living space, and some because the air conditioner simply cannot keep up with latent load. One unit does not suit all three, so the six picks cover ducted, ventilating and low-temperature basement designs, and the sections below explain which kind of house each one fits.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have not yet decided whether you need a ducted unit at all, start with our comparison of <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/">whole house versus portable dehumidifiers</a>. For many houses with one damp basement, a good portable from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> is the cheaper answer, and that page models the fifteen-year cost of both routes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ducted, Ventilating or Basement-First
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standard ducted whole house dehumidifier sits in a basement, attic or utility room and connects to the HVAC system, usually pulling air from the return side and pushing dried air back into the supply or return. The Aprilaire E100 and E070 and the Honeywell DR90 are this kind. They use the existing duct system to spread dry air through the house, which is efficient, but they depend on that system's airflow being designed for them, and the install has to be balanced against the furnace or air handler.
          </p>

          <p>
            A ventilating dehumidifier adds a fresh-air intake. The Santa Fe Ultra V100 and Ultra70 can draw a measured stream of outdoor air, filter it and dry it before it enters the house. That matters in very airtight new construction, where the house needs mechanical ventilation anyway and outdoor air arrives humid in summer. In a leaky older house, the ventilation port adds little, because the house is already getting plenty of outside air through gaps you did not plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            A basement-first unit, like the AlorAir Sentinel HDi90, is really a high-capacity basement and crawl space dehumidifier that can be ducted. It is the right tool when the moisture source is below grade, which is common. Drying the basement or crawl space often lowers humidity upstairs too, and our article on whether <a href="/will-a-basement-dehumidifier-help-the-whole-house/">a basement dehumidifier helps the whole house</a> explains when that works and when it does not. These units also run at lower temperatures than the ducted machines, which matters in a cold basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            All of them are refrigerant designs: a cold coil condenses water out of the air, and the water runs to a drain. None of them is a desiccant unit, and none of them heats the air the way a desiccant machine does. That makes them efficient at normal indoor temperatures and progressively weaker as the air gets colder, which is why the realistic ratings in the next section matter so much.
          </p>
        </div>

