          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a whole house dehumidifier worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it when the whole house is humid, not just one room. Equipment typically runs about $1,200 to $2,500 plus installation, so for a single damp basement a portable is usually cheaper. In a humid climate where every room feels sticky, one ducted unit replaces several portables and dries the house more evenly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a whole house dehumidifier cost installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Equipment typically runs about $1,200 to $2,500. A standalone install with its own short duct runs commonly adds $500 to $1,200, and integrating it with the HVAC system commonly adds $800 to $1,500. These are typical ranges, not quotes, and local labor rates and duct complexity move them a lot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size whole house dehumidifier do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Size on the capacity at about 73°F, not the 80°F headline, and on measured humidity rather than square footage alone. A house needing about 60 to 70 pints a day in midsummer usually wants a 100-pint-class unit. A contractor's load calculation beats any square-foot chart.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a whole house dehumidifier work without ductwork?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can run standalone in a basement with their own short supply duct, which dries that space and some of the house above it. To dry every room evenly, though, the unit normally connects to the HVAC ducts. Basement-first units like the AlorAir HDi90 are sold for exactly this standalone role.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I set a whole house dehumidifier to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 45 to 50 percent relative humidity suits most homes. Below about 40 percent you are paying to remove moisture you do not need to, and above about 55 to 60 percent mold and dust mites thrive. Start at 50 and adjust after a few weeks of hygrometer readings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does a whole house dehumidifier use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ENERGY STAR lists the Santa Fe Ultra V100 at 419 kWh a year, and Santa Fe gives its draw as about 620 watts at 73°F. Multiply the wattage by your running hours and electricity rate for your own estimate. These units remove more water per kilowatt-hour than most portables.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do whole house dehumidifiers need a drain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. At 50 to 100 pints a day, a bucket is not practical, so the unit drains to a floor drain, utility sink or condensate pump. The drain line needs a steady slope, any trap the manufacturer requires and protection from freezing if it passes through an unheated space.
              </p>
            </div>

